<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Amalfi</h1><p class="st-meta">839 CE – 1131 CE (approx.) · capital Amalfi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Maritime republic on the Sorrento peninsula whose merchants traded with Constantinople, Egypt and Jerusalem in the 10th and 11th centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Amalfi was a small fortified town on the steep coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorrento">Sorrento</a> peninsula, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-naples">Byzantine Duchy of Naples</a>. In 839, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicard-of-benevento">Sicard of Benevento</a> had attacked it and taken its people away, the Amalfitans broke from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and elected their own leaders, first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prefect">prefects</a> and later judges.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>From 958 Amalfi was ruled by dukes, among them Manso I, who also controlled <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-salerno">Salerno</a>. With little farmland, it lived from trade: its merchants had colonies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-antioch">Antioch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and founded a hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> from which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a> later emerged. It minted gold tari, and the maritime code known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabula-amalphitana">Tabula Amalphitana</a> was used across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard">Robert Guiscard</a> took Amalfi in 1073. It revolted and was absorbed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II</a>'s <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily</a> in 1131. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> sacked it in 1135 and 1137, and a storm in 1343 destroyed much of the lower town. Its cathedral and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalfi-coast">Amalfi Coast</a> are now famous.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-amalfi.jpg" alt="Republic of Amalfi" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Oren neu dag (talk) The SVG code is valid. This vector image was created with Inkscape-default · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Republic_of_Amalfi.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>839 CE – 1131 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Amalfi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Manso I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Duchy of Amalfi, Amalfi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=985&amp;focus=republic-of-amalfi" aria-label="Republic of Amalfi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5038 726 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Amalfi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5358 982L5349 976M5358 982L5362 981M5358 982L5383 971M5358 982L5333 1064"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5349 976h0M5362 981h0M5383 971h0M5333 1064h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5358 982h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5358 982h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Amalfi. <a href="/atlas?year=985&amp;focus=republic-of-amalfi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
